{
  "dom_id": "step-q163796",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/16e40e931919d695fe867f128827bc5351e390bb712009bfba130d195f5f35a2.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ of mass $10\\,kg$ and $8kg$, respectively, are connected by a rigid, massless rod that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is $5\\,m,$ find the distance between the fulcrum and $A.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $d$ be the distance between the fulcrum and $A,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/6df23f8b7fd4202667daa23b1dc110bb1d546516fd37a2abd208b9c1c84b79c4.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-10\\cdot d=-10d\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $8\\cdot 5=40\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe *principle of moments* tells us that for the two masses to perfectly balance, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40-10d & =0 \\\\ 10d & =40 \\\\ d & =4\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
